response:
[
  {
    "heading": "1.1 Coverage",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Most standard property policies, including many homeowners policies, do not cover damage caused by **flood**. That means a separate flood insurance policy is typically needed if your client wants protection for losses caused by rising water. Most flood coverage is obtained through the National Flood Insurance Program, often called the **NFIP**, or through private insurers that issue NFIP policies under the Write-Your-Own program. In practice, the policy terms still follow the NFIP form even when a private carrier services the policy."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "A central point in flood insurance is that not every water loss is a flood. For NFIP purposes, a loss must meet a specific policy definition before coverage applies. If the event does not fit that definition, the claim is handled under some other policy if coverage exists there. This distinction is important when you explain expectations to clients after a water-related loss."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading_3",
        "content": "What the NFIP considers a flood"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Under the NFIP, a covered **flood** is a general and temporary condition where water partially or completely covers normally dry land. The event must affect at least two acres of land or at least two properties, and one of those properties can be the insured property. This requirement helps distinguish a broad flood event from a localized household water problem. The source of the water must also fall within the policy definition."
      },
      {
        "type": "bullet_list",
        "items": [
          "Overflow of inland waters or tidal waters",
          "Unusual and rapid accumulation or runoff of surface waters from any source",
          "Mudflow, meaning flowing mud caused by flooding rather than a simple landslide",
          "Collapse or subsidence of land along a lake or similar body of water when erosion or undermining from waves or currents causes flooding conditions"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The reference to **mudflow** often needs explanation. It means a river-like flow of liquid mud over normally dry land, where water carries earth along its path. It does not mean every earth movement event is covered. A dry landslide, for example, is not the same as a flood-related mudflow under the NFIP definition."
      },
      {
        "type": "important_callout",
        "content": "A flood policy responds only when the loss meets the NFIP definition of flood. Many water losses feel like floods to a homeowner, but they may not qualify under the policy."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "This definition matters because clients often use the word flood in a general way. If a basement fills with water because a sump pump fails, that event usually does not satisfy the NFIP definition. It is a water loss, but not an NFIP flood. You would not automatically look to the flood policy for payment in that situation."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The same issue can arise with sewer backup, seepage, or plumbing leaks. Those losses may be serious and expensive, but flood insurance uses its own defined trigger. You need to connect the cause of loss to the policy definition before discussing available benefits. This helps avoid confusion at claim time."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading_3",
        "content": "Main categories of coverage"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The Standard Flood Insurance Policy, or **SFIP**, is organized around several main coverage categories. These categories help you separate what the policy protects, what special limits apply, and when deductibles are used. The four broad areas are building property, personal property, other coverages, and increased cost of compliance. Each category serves a different purpose in the overall policy structure."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading_4",
        "content": "Building property"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**Building property** coverage applies to the insured structure itself. This generally includes the dwelling and attached additions or extensions. A detached garage may also be covered, but only up to 10 percent of the building coverage limit. That detached garage amount does not increase the building limit; it comes from it."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Building coverage can also apply while a structure is under construction, alteration, or repair. Materials and supplies that are to be used for the work may be included if they are properly stored. This means coverage is not limited only to a finished, occupied building. You still need to review the policy form for details on what property qualifies."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading_4",
        "content": "Personal property"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**Personal property** coverage protects contents kept in a fully enclosed and secure insured building. This can include furniture, clothing, electronics, and similar contents, subject to policy terms. Coverage may also extend to insured personal property that is removed to safety because a flood is approaching. That extension is time limited and generally lasts up to 45 days."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Some classes of contents are subject to a special cap even when contents coverage is purchased. Examples include jewelry, artwork, furs, rare books, and similar valuable items. For these categories, the policy limit is not unlimited within the contents amount. Instead, a special limit of $2,500 applies."
      },
      {
        "type": "important_callout",
        "content": "Contents coverage can include property moved to safety before a flood, but only for a limited time and subject to policy terms. Valuable items such as jewelry and artwork are also subject to a separate special limit."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading_4",
        "content": "Other coverages"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**Other coverages** include additional benefits that support the main building or contents protection. These benefits can vary depending on which SFIP form is used, such as the Dwelling Form, the General Property Form, or the Residential Condominium Building Association Policy form. Although they are not the primary insuring agreement, they can be important in a loss. You should understand them as separate grants of coverage with their own rules."
      },
      {
        "type": "bullet_list",
        "items": [
          "Debris removal after a covered flood loss",
          "Reasonable measures taken to avoid or reduce loss when a flood threat is imminent",
          "Expenses to move insured property to a safer location",
          "Condominium loss assessments in situations allowed by the policy form",
          "Pollution damage in situations covered under the applicable form"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "These supplemental coverages can be especially helpful when a client acts before or after a flood to reduce damage. For example, moving insured contents to safety or buying temporary protective materials may fall within policy allowances. Still, these are not open-ended benefits. Payment depends on the policy form and the limits that apply to each item."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading_4",
        "content": "Increased cost of compliance"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**Increased cost of compliance**, often shortened to **ICC**, helps with extra costs that arise when a damaged building must be brought into compliance with current floodplain management rules. In plain terms, this coverage addresses expenses created by ordinance or law after a flood loss. It does not simply pay for ordinary repair. Instead, it helps when the community requires changes as part of rebuilding."
      },
      {
        "type": "bullet_list",
        "items": [
          "Elevation of the building",
          "Floodproofing for eligible nonresidential buildings and certain residential buildings with approved basements",
          "Relocation of the structure",
          "Demolition when required to comply with local rules"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "ICC is important because rebuilding after a serious flood can trigger compliance requirements that increase project cost. If local rules require the building to be elevated or otherwise modified, the insured may face expenses beyond simple repair work. ICC is designed to respond to that added burden within policy limits. It is a separate coverage concept from direct physical flood damage."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading_3",
        "content": "How deductibles apply"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Flood policies apply **deductibles**, which are the amounts the insured pays before policy benefits are paid. Under the SFIP, building property and personal property have separate deductibles. If both the structure and contents are damaged in the same covered flood, both deductibles apply. This is a common testing point and an important claim discussion item."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "For example, if a policy has one deductible for the building and another for contents, the insured does not pay only the lower amount or only the higher amount. The building loss is adjusted with the building deductible, and the contents loss is adjusted with the contents deductible. You need to think of them as two distinct parts of the same claim. That approach follows the policy structure."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Not every flood-related payment is subject to a deductible. The policy states that deductibles do not apply to certain benefits, including loss avoidance measures, condominium loss assessments, and ICC claims. That exception matters because it changes how reimbursement is calculated for those items. You should separate those benefits from the main building and contents loss calculations."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "When you explain flood coverage, focus on the policy’s defined terms and categories. Clients often think first about the damage they can see, but coverage begins with how the event is classified under the NFIP form. From there, you can identify whether the claim involves the building, contents, supplemental benefits, or compliance-related costs. A clear understanding of those coverage parts makes later discussions about limits and rates much easier."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "1.2 Coverage Limits",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Flood coverage through the **NFIP** is not available everywhere automatically. To qualify, the insured property must be in a participating community or on eligible federal land. A participating community is one that agrees to follow NFIP floodplain management standards. This participation is important because the program ties insurance availability to local efforts to reduce future flood damage."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Before a community can participate, it must adopt a **floodplain management ordinance**. This is a local rule that governs how land in flood-prone areas may be developed or improved. The community must also have an official flood map, such as a **Flood Insurance Rate Map** or **FIRM**, or a Flood Hazard Boundary Map. These maps identify areas with flood exposure and support the administration of the program."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading_3",
        "content": "Emergency Program and Regular Program"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Community participation begins with the **Emergency Program**. This is the initial phase of NFIP participation, and coverage is available at lower maximum amounts. It allows insurance to be written while the community completes the steps needed for full program status. Because the limits are reduced, this phase offers less protection than the later program stage."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Once the FIRM is fully in effect, the community moves into the **Regular Program**. At that point, higher coverage limits are available for eligible property types. This change matters because the amount your client can purchase depends not only on the building type, but also on the community’s program status. You cannot assume the full NFIP limits apply in every participating area."
      },
      {
        "type": "important_callout",
        "content": "NFIP eligibility depends on community participation. Even when flood exposure exists, available limits depend on whether the community is in the Emergency Program or the Regular Program."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Under the Emergency Program, residential building coverage is available up to $35,000 and contents coverage up to $10,000. Small business and other nonresidential buildings can obtain up to $100,000 for the building and $100,000 for contents. Other residential buildings have their own Emergency Program limits as well, but the key point is that they are lower than the limits available later. You should always verify the occupancy category before discussing available amounts."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Under the Regular Program, a single-family dwelling can generally obtain up to $250,000 of building coverage and up to $100,000 of contents coverage. Other residential and nonresidential occupancies may have different maximums, often up to $500,000 for building and $500,000 for contents in eligible categories. These higher amounts reflect the community’s full participation status. They do not change the policy form’s other terms, conditions, or exclusions."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading_3",
        "content": "Limits on additional coverages"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**Increased cost of compliance** coverage also has a limit. The maximum ICC amount is $30,000. However, ICC does not sit on top of the building limit without restriction. When building damage and ICC are both paid, the combined amount cannot exceed the statutory maximum available for building coverage under the NFIP."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "For example, if a single-family home carries the maximum $250,000 building limit and a covered flood results in a $220,000 building payment, the full $30,000 of ICC could still be available. But if the building payment is $230,000, only $20,000 of ICC could be paid because the total cannot exceed $250,000. This is a coordination rule, not a separate expansion of limits. You should treat ICC as part of the overall building coverage cap when both apply."
      },
      {
        "type": "bullet_list",
        "items": [
          "$1,000 for reasonable loss avoidance measures taken because flood is imminent, plus the value of the insured’s work subject to the federal minimum wage standard",
          "$1,000 for reasonable expenses to move insured property to safety",
          "$10,000 for covered pollution damage under the General Property Form"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "important_callout",
        "content": "Some NFIP benefits have their own dollar caps. ICC, loss avoidance, property removal, and pollution damage are not unlimited and may also interact with other policy limits."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "When you discuss limits with clients, keep three questions in mind: Is the community participating, which program phase applies, and what occupancy type is insured? Those factors determine the maximum coverage available before you even review deductibles or premium. A flood policy may provide meaningful protection, but the exact amount available depends on the NFIP structure. Understanding these limits prepares you for later discussions about how rates are developed."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "1.3 Rates",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Flood insurance pricing has changed over time as lawmakers and regulators have tried to address affordability and long-term program stability. Those efforts have not fully resolved every concern, but they have shaped how the National Flood Insurance Program prices policies today. The current pricing approach is called **Risk Rating 2.0**. This method focuses more directly on the unique characteristics of each property and its flood exposure."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Under earlier methods, flood premiums relied heavily on broad flood zones and **base flood elevation**, often shortened to **BFE**. Base flood elevation is a measurement tied to the level that floodwater is expected to reach during a flood of a specified severity. That earlier approach grouped many properties together based mainly on mapped zones and elevation relationships. Risk Rating 2.0 moves away from depending on those traditional classifications as the primary rating basis."
      },
      {
        "type": "important_callout",
        "content": "Risk Rating 2.0 prices flood insurance by looking at property-specific risk characteristics rather than relying mainly on older zone-based and BFE-centered methods."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "This shift is not unusual when you compare flood insurance to other insurance lines. In many kinds of insurance, the premium reflects the risk characteristics of the individual applicant or property. For example, auto premiums often vary based on driving history, and property insurance premiums may reflect structural features that affect the chance or severity of loss. What is newer is the NFIP’s stronger move toward that individualized model."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "For your study purposes, Risk Rating 2.0 can be understood through three broad rating categories: location, building characteristics, and policy coverage. These categories organize the main ideas behind pricing. They do not mean only three facts are reviewed. Instead, each category includes several detailed variables that together influence premium."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading_3",
        "content": "Location factors"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**Location** is a major part of flood risk because water exposure is tied closely to where the building sits. Risk Rating 2.0 looks beyond a simple flood zone label. It considers how often flooding may occur, what kind of flooding may affect the property, and how close the structure is to the source of floodwater. These details help create a more property-specific picture of risk."
      },
      {
        "type": "bullet_list",
        "items": [
          "Flood frequency, meaning how often flooding conditions may affect the area",
          "Flood type, such as river overflow, coastal storm surge, heavy rainfall, or erosion-related conditions",
          "Distance to the water source, which may influence how strongly the property is exposed",
          "Ground elevation of the building site and nearby land",
          "Characteristics of adjacent flooding sources and surrounding terrain"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "These location variables matter because not all flood hazards behave in the same way. A property near a river may face different conditions than a property exposed to storm surge from coastal waters. Likewise, rainfall-driven flooding may create a different loss pattern than erosion along a shoreline. Risk Rating 2.0 attempts to reflect those distinctions rather than treating all mapped properties alike."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Distance to water source is one example of a more detailed rating factor. Two buildings may be in the same general community, but one may sit much closer to a river, bay, or other flood source. That closer proximity can affect expected loss potential. The revised rating method accounts for that kind of difference in a way older broad classifications often did not."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Ground elevation also remains relevant, but in a different way than older rating systems used it. Under prior methods, a major question was often how the structure related to a mapped BFE. Under Risk Rating 2.0, the program considers elevation as part of a larger property-specific analysis. The key testing point is that BFEs themselves are no longer the main rating tool."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading_3",
        "content": "Building characteristics"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**Building characteristics** describe how the structure is built and how that design affects flood damage potential. Some features make a building more vulnerable to water intrusion or more expensive to repair after a loss. Others may reduce damage by raising living space above likely floodwater or by allowing water to move through lower areas more safely. These physical traits are an important part of pricing."
      },
      {
        "type": "bullet_list",
        "items": [
          "Foundation type, including whether the building is elevated",
          "First-floor height or lowest floor position relative to expected flood exposure",
          "Presence of a basement or lower enclosed area",
          "Number of stories, which can spread value and occupancy vertically",
          "Construction type, such as masonry versus wood frame",
          "Flood openings that allow water to flow through certain enclosed areas",
          "Replacement cost value, meaning the estimated cost to repair or rebuild with materials of like kind and quality"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "An elevated building often presents less flood risk than a building with a low first floor or a basement. If important building systems and occupied living areas are raised above likely floodwater, direct damage may be reduced. In contrast, basements and low-lying levels can be more susceptible to water entry and more severe damage. That increased vulnerability can affect premium."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Construction materials also matter. Masonry may perform differently in flood conditions than a wood-frame structure. The rating system considers how a building is likely to withstand water exposure and what repairs may cost afterward. This does not mean one material is always rated the same way in every scenario, but it does mean structural composition influences loss expectations."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The number of stories can also influence pricing. A building with more than one story may distribute its value across multiple levels. In a flood, lower portions may still sustain damage, but all of the building’s value may not be concentrated at the lowest level. That can change the expected severity of loss compared with a single-level structure where more of the property is exposed near the ground."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Flood openings are another useful example. These are designed openings that allow floodwaters to enter and exit certain enclosed areas in a controlled way. By helping equalize pressure, they may reduce structural stress and damage in some situations. Their presence can therefore affect how the building’s flood exposure is evaluated."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Replacement cost value is especially important because pricing reflects not only the chance of a flood, but also the likely size of a covered loss. A building that costs more to repair or rebuild generally represents a larger potential claim. For that reason, higher-value structures often carry higher flood premiums than less expensive structures with otherwise similar exposure. The premium reflects expected loss cost, not just flood frequency."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading_3",
        "content": "Occupancy and building use"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The kind of building being insured also affects rates. A single-family home, a residential condominium building, and a nonresidential commercial structure may not be priced the same way. Different occupancy types face different patterns of use, valuation, and covered property exposure. They may also qualify for different forms or coverage maximums under the NFIP."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Occupancy influences both what can be insured and how the risk is viewed. For example, a building used as a residence may be treated differently from one used for business operations. Even where the location hazard is similar, the expected damage pattern, contents profile, and cost to restore functionality may differ. Those distinctions help explain why building type is part of rating."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "When you review a flood application, you are not just asking where the property is. You are also identifying what the building is and how it is used. Those facts connect directly to available policy forms, maximum limits, and premium factors. In that sense, eligibility, coverage design, and rating are closely related."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading_3",
        "content": "Policy coverage choices"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**Policy coverage** refers to the choices made within the policy itself, especially coverage limits and deductibles. These choices directly affect premium because they change how much risk the insurer retains and how much remains with the policyholder. In general, more coverage means more premium because the policy may pay more after a covered loss. Less coverage may reduce premium, but it also reduces the amount available for payment."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Coverage limits are a straightforward rating factor. If a policyholder buys a higher building limit or contents limit, the insurer is potentially exposed to a larger claim. That larger possible payout generally leads to a higher premium. This reflects the basic insurance principle that broader financial protection usually costs more."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**Deductible** choice also affects rate. A deductible is the amount the insured pays before policy benefits begin for a covered loss. When a higher deductible is selected, the policyholder retains more of the initial loss amount. Because the insurer is responsible for less of the smaller and moderate loss range, the premium will typically be lower than it would be with a smaller deductible."
      },
      {
        "type": "important_callout",
        "content": "Premium is influenced not only by hazard, but also by coverage design. Higher limits usually increase cost, while higher deductibles generally reduce it."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "This relationship between limits and deductibles is important when you compare policies. Two insureds in similar locations may pay different premiums because they purchased different amounts of building or contents coverage, or because they chose different deductibles. The rating result is therefore not based only on the map or the building itself. Policy structure matters too."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "You should also remember that flood deductibles can apply separately to building and contents losses. That feature affects claims rather than the initial hazard rating, but it is still tied to policy design. Clients may assume one deductible applies to the entire loss, yet the SFIP treats building and personal property as separate coverage parts. A rate discussion is clearer when that claim structure is already understood."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading_3",
        "content": "Why the newer method matters"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Risk Rating 2.0 matters because it aims to align premium more closely with the actual characteristics of each insured risk. Under a broad zone-based model, properties with meaningful differences in exposure could sometimes appear more alike than they really were. The revised methodology seeks to account for those differences in a more refined way. That is why the system reviews many details about location, structure, and coverage."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "For insurance professionals, this means flood pricing discussions may require more explanation than a simple reference to the property’s zone. A client may ask why a premium changed even though the property did not move. The answer may involve updated rating variables, replacement cost considerations, or building features that are now weighted differently. Your role is to explain that the NFIP now emphasizes individual flood risk more directly."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "It also means older assumptions about what drives flood premium may no longer be fully accurate. Someone familiar with BFEs and traditional zone-based rates may expect those concepts to control every pricing decision. Under the revised method, they are not the central basis for rating in the way they once were. The focus has shifted toward a broader set of property-specific indicators."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "That broader perspective does not remove the importance of maps or community participation. Those elements still matter within the NFIP framework. However, when you study rates specifically, you need to separate eligibility and general program administration from the newer pricing method. Risk Rating 2.0 is about how the premium is developed once the property is eligible for coverage."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading_3",
        "content": "Putting the rating factors together"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "A practical way to understand rates is to think in layers. First, consider the property’s exposure to floodwater based on location. Next, consider how the building’s design and value may affect the extent and cost of damage. Then consider how much coverage is being purchased and how much loss the insured retains through deductibles. The final premium reflects all of those ideas working together."
      },
      {
        "type": "bullet_list",
        "items": [
          "Location helps estimate how likely flooding is and what kind of flooding may occur",
          "Building characteristics help estimate how severely the property may be damaged",
          "Replacement cost helps estimate how expensive the loss may be to repair",
          "Coverage limits affect the maximum amount the policy may have to pay",
          "Deductibles affect how much of the loss remains with the insured"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "This layered approach also explains why no single factor tells the whole story. A property may be close to water but elevated well above likely flood levels. Another may be farther from the water source but built in a way that increases damage severity if flooding occurs. A complete premium reflects the interaction of multiple factors, not just one headline feature."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "When you discuss these topics in an educational setting, keep your explanation neutral and factual. The purpose is to help learners understand how the NFIP approaches pricing. You are describing how premiums are influenced by risk and coverage choices, not suggesting what any person ought to purchase. That distinction is especially important in compliance-focused training."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "In summary, Risk Rating 2.0 uses a more individualized approach to flood insurance rates. It reviews where the property is located, how the building is constructed, what the building is worth to repair or rebuild, and what coverage terms are selected. It no longer relies primarily on older BFE-based pricing methods. Understanding that framework will help you connect coverage, limits, and premium in a consistent way."
      },
      {
        "type": "knowledge_check",
        "question": "Which of the following factors is NOT used under the NFIP’s revised Risk Rating 2.0 methodology to price risks?",
        "options": [
          "A) Distance to water source",
          "B) Replacement cost value",
          "C) Flood type",
          "D) Base flood elevations (BFEs)"
        ],
        "correct_answer": "D",
        "explanation": "D is correct because Risk Rating 2.0 no longer relies on BFEs as the primary pricing basis. The revised method instead considers property-specific factors such as distance to the water source, replacement cost value, and the types of flooding that may affect the location."
      }
    ]
  }
]

[
  {
    "heading": "2.1 Coverage for Floods in Urban Areas",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Many people assume cities are safer from flooding, but urban areas often face serious **flood** exposure. Two common threats are **flash flooding**, which begins within hours of intense rain or infrastructure failure, and urban flooding, which happens when drainage systems cannot handle runoff. These events can develop very quickly."
      },
      {
        "type": "bullet_list",
        "items": [
          "Flood speed and severity can change based on rainfall intensity and where the rain falls.",
          "Land use, slope, vegetation, soil type, and how wet the soil already is also matter.",
          "Concrete, pavement, and roads prevent water from soaking into the ground."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Cities also change constantly. Construction may remove green space before new drainage is fully in place, which can increase runoff. Older sewers and other aging systems may overflow more easily during major storms."
      },
      {
        "type": "important_callout",
        "content": "Urban flood risk is not limited to coastal neighborhoods. Dense development and impervious surfaces can make inland city areas highly vulnerable too."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Large metropolitan areas may also face storm surge, hurricanes, and changing weather patterns that intensify rainfall. Because flooding in cities can be fast and severe, understanding urban exposure is an important part of discussing flood insurance coverage."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "2.1.1 A Case Study in Urban Flood Insurance",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "A useful **case study** comes from Long Beach, New York, a dense coastal community within the New York metro area. NFIP data showed it had the largest number of single-family flood policies in force for its ZIP code as of August 31, 2023. The median full-risk cost was higher than the median amount actually paid after discounts or subsidies."
      },
