    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**Affordability** remains one of the NFIP’s hardest problems. As you have seen, lawmakers and FEMA have tried different approaches, but the same tension remains: keeping premiums manageable while also keeping the program financially sound. Risk Rating 2.0 addressed pricing fairness, yet you should recognize that fairer pricing can still create serious cost concerns."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.2.1 Will Risk Rating 2.0 Actually Improve Rates?",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "FEMA has said Risk Rating 2.0 should make premiums fairer by using better data, modern actuarial methods, and property-specific risk factors. In theory, you would expect that approach to reduce cross-subsidies and improve the program’s financial position. FEMA also projected that many existing policyholders would see either decreases or modest monthly increases."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Still, you should not assume affordability problems are solved. Critics argue that higher full-risk pricing and subsidy phaseouts may hit low- and moderate-income households hardest, encourage lower limits or dropped coverage, and worsen adverse selection, meaning higher-risk property owners stay insured while lower-risk owners leave."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.2.2 Proposals for Improving Flood Insurance Affordability",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Several **proposals** aim to improve affordability without ignoring risk. FEMA’s proposed Homeowner Flood Form could help by letting you and your clients match coverage choices more closely to actual needs. Congress and the U.S. Government Accountability Office have also discussed broader reforms that would move assistance away from hidden premium discounts and toward more transparent support."
      },
      {
        "type": "bullet_list",
        "items": [
          "Create a means-based affordability program",
          "Expand participation to spread costs across a larger insured pool",
          "Reduce NFIP debt or change how catastrophic losses are financed",
          "Increase mitigation and improve property-specific risk communication"
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.3 Congressional Reauthorization of the NFIP",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Congress must periodically **reauthorize** the NFIP so it can continue operating. For you, that matters because repeated short-term extensions create uncertainty for policyholders, insurers, and communities. Since long-term renewal ended in 2017, Congress has relied on short extensions and temporary lapses, while larger reform proposals have remained under review."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.3.1 Financial Framework",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The proposed **financial framework** changes focus on making the NFIP more stable. You would see stronger support for full-risk rates, lower borrowing authority tied to expected premiums, and a structure that combines premiums, reserve funds, risk transfer, and limited borrowing so FEMA can pay claims more reliably after major flood events."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.3.2 Risk Analysis and Communication",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Proposals on **risk analysis** would modernize maps and disclosures. You should expect stronger flood-risk communication, including climate-informed mapping, seller and lessor reporting standards in residential transactions, replacement cost value in rating, and clearer distinctions between coastal and inland flood exposures so premiums better reflect actual conditions."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.3.3 Resilience",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**Resilience** proposals aim to reduce future losses while improving access. They include a longer NFIP extension, a means-tested assistance program for low- and moderate-income households, revised treatment of repetitive-loss properties, higher coverage limits, and restrictions on some new high-risk construction, all intended to strengthen communities and reduce long-term flood costs."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.3.4 Technical and Operational Enhancements",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**Technical enhancements** would change how the program operates. You could see a required administrative appeal before suit, a shorter deadline for lawsuits after appeal decisions, fewer required reports with timelier information, and easier movement between NFIP and private flood coverage when continuous coverage rules would otherwise create barriers."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.4. The Need for Model Laws in the Private Market",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "As private flood insurance grows, **model laws** can help state insurance regulators create more consistent rules. You benefit from that consistency because it can support clearer standards for insurers and better consumer protections. The NAIC has offered guidance, and NCOIL adopted a model act to help states build private residential flood markets."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.4.1 The National Association of Insurance Commissioners",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The **NAIC** has not adopted a private flood model law, but it has given state insurance regulators guidance on how to support this market. In its 2019 considerations document, it discussed legislative changes, consumer information, and actions by agents and lenders. For you, the practical takeaway is that states may streamline rate and form filing, improve consumer outreach, collect better flood data, and encourage more producer education so clients better understand flood risk and available coverage options."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.4.2 The National Council of Insurance Legislators",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "In 2020, **NCOIL** adopted the Private Primary Residential Flood Insurance Model Act. If your state uses it as a guide, you may see rules on rates, forms, insurer notices, consumer disclosures, cancellation and nonrenewal, and surplus lines placements. The act is meant to support a workable private flood market while preserving state regulatory control."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.4.3 ISO Flood Insurance Programs",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**ISO** introduced private flood programs for commercial lines in 2017 and personal lines in 2018. These forms give insurers tools for underwriting and rating. For you, that means private carriers may have structured options beyond the NFIP, including broader commercial coverages, flexible limits, and pricing supported by flood territories and modeling."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.5 Mitigation at the Community and State Levels",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**Mitigation** can lower future flood losses and support affordability. FEMA offers hazard mitigation assistance grants for states, communities, territories, and tribal nations. When you think about long-term solutions, these programs matter because they fund projects before and after disasters, helping communities reduce risk and, over time, ease pressure on insurance costs."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.5.1 Actions for State and Local Governments",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "States often administer **grant** funds and help local governments set mitigation priorities. You may see efforts such as updated building codes, zoning controls, revolving loan funds, or tax incentives for qualifying work in Special Flood Hazard Areas. These choices can shape which projects move forward and how quickly communities improve flood resilience."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.5.2 Options for Communities",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Communities have several **options** for mitigation support. Before disasters, they may pursue BRIC or Flood Mitigation Assistance grants. After disasters, they may use the Hazard Mitigation Grant Program or HMGP Post-Fire for secondary hazards such as flooding after wildfire. Communities can also join the Community Rating System and earn uniform NFIP premium discounts when they exceed minimum floodplain standards."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.5.3 Safeguarding Tomorrow Revolving Loan Fund (RLF) Program",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The **Safeguarding Tomorrow Revolving Loan Fund** program helps states, territories, tribal governments, and Washington, D.C., create loan funds for local mitigation projects. You should know that these low-interest loans are meant to support communities with limited resources, and FEMA aims to direct a meaningful share of benefits to under-resourced areas."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Unlike many hazard mitigation grants, revolving loan funds can give local governments more flexibility. Loans may also help meet nonfederal cost-share requirements for FEMA mitigation grants, and the program generally does not require a benefit-cost analysis for each project."
      }
    ]
  }
]

[
  {
    "heading": "4.1 A Brief History of Climate-Related Insurance Losses",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "From 1980 forward, you can see climate-related insurance losses climbing as severe storms, floods, and coastal events became more costly. Private insurers paid most weather claims, while the NFIP covered major flood losses. Growth in risky development also mattered, but rising climate stress has clearly increased pressure on insurance systems."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "4.2 The Impact on Flood Risks",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Before you assess climate effects, you should recall how flood risk is measured. **Special Flood Hazard Areas** are FEMA map zones beginning with A or V. They carry at least a 1 percent annual flood chance, or about a one-in-four chance over a 30-year mortgage. Climate change is expected to expand these high-risk areas."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "4.2.1 The Impact on Inland Flooding",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "For inland flooding, you should expect broader river floodplains and heavier rainfall. National projections show riverine high-risk areas growing about 45 percent by 2100, with most of that increase tied to climate change rather than population alone. Warmer air holds more moisture, wetter winters shift snow to rain, and drought-hardened soil can worsen runoff when storms finally arrive."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "4.2.2 The Impact on Coastal Flooding",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Along the coast, you need to watch sea level rise and storm behavior together. If shorelines stay in place, coastal high-risk zones may expand sharply; if they recede, growth may be smaller. More frequent storms also shorten the practical meaning of a “100-year flood,” raising the yearly chance that damaging water reaches insured property."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "4.3 The Impact on the NFIP",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "For the **NFIP**, you can expect more policies, higher average loss costs, and higher premiums as high-risk areas expand. Projections differ by shoreline conditions, but both scenarios point upward. Because these estimates predated Risk Rating 2.0, exact figures may shift, yet the overall direction still signals growing program pressure."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "4.4 The Impact on Private Flood Insurance",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "You have likely seen private flood insurance become more visible in recent years. Higher NFIP prices, better catastrophe modeling, and growing demand have encouraged carriers to reenter a market they once avoided after catastrophic losses. For you and your clients, this may mean more options, but state insurance regulators still must watch solvency, disclosures, and rating practices carefully."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "4.5 The Impact on Urban Areas",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "When you bring these trends together, urban areas stand out. Dense development, older infrastructure, and large concentrations of people can turn climate-driven flooding into a wider insurance and public safety problem. You will see that risk, repair costs, and affordability pressures often rise together in cities."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "4.5.1 The Impact on Urban Flood Risk",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "In cities, you may face both coastal and inland flood pressure. Some populous coastal regions are projected to see local sea level rise exceed global averages. Inland cities also face growing losses from heavier rainfall and runoff. You should also note that lower-income neighborhoods are often at lower elevations and at greater flood risk."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "4.5.2 The Impact on Urban Infrastructure",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "You cannot separate urban flood risk from infrastructure. Aging storm drains, canals, dams, and levees are under strain, and some systems were not built for today’s rainfall patterns or sea levels. Even helpful upgrades like green stormwater features have limits. As state insurance regulators know, weak infrastructure can increase losses and slow recovery."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "4.5.3 The Impact on Urban Flood Premiums",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "As climate risk grows in cities, you should expect premium pressure to grow too. Earlier projections for NFIP pricing pointed upward, and broader property insurance studies show similar trends. Large cities with severe flood exposure may see especially sharp increases, which can make affordability a bigger issue for your clients."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "4.6 The Impact on Under-Resourced Communities",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Climate change does not affect all communities the same way. You should recognize that under-resourced communities often have fewer tools to prepare, respond, and recover. They may face weaker housing, limited infrastructure, and fewer financial reserves, which can make flood losses, displacement, and insurance barriers much more severe."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "4.6.1 The Disproportionate Exposures",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "You will often see higher exposure where affordable housing is older, lower, or poorly protected. EPA findings show some communities of color, Indigenous communities, low-income households, and people with limited English proficiency are more likely to live in places with greater flood danger and fewer adaptation resources."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "4.6.2 The Matter of Recovery",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Recovery can be as unequal as exposure. After floods, you may see under-resourced communities wait longer for power restoration and repairs, especially when utilities first prioritize critical or commercial areas. Longer outages can spoil food, interrupt medical care, delay cleanup, and worsen property damage, making recovery slower and more expensive for households already under strain."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "4.6.3 The Vicious Cycle",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "You can think of this as a cycle: higher climate risk can reduce property values, strain local resources, raise insurance costs, and push coverage further out of reach. Some neighborhoods may keep growing despite risk, while others lose residents. For under-resourced communities, each step can reinforce the next and deepen instability."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "4.7 The NAIC National Climate Resilience Strategy",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "In 2024, the **NAIC** adopted a National Climate Resilience Strategy to help state insurance regulators respond faster to climate risk. If you work with property coverage, this matters because the strategy supports mitigation, better public risk information, stronger solvency tools, and more consistent regulatory planning across different states and hazards."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "4.7.1 Action 1: Close Gaps",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Under Action 1, you should focus on protection gaps. The strategy calls for tools that show where coverage is missing, training for state insurance regulators, and stronger communication for policyholders and the public. It also encourages local governments to include insurance access when they plan mitigation projects."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "4.7.2 Action 2: Flood Insurance Blueprint",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Action 2 asks states to build a broader flood insurance blueprint instead of relying only on the NFIP. You would see more public education, stronger support for private flood insurance, local research partnerships, better communication with governments, and closer coordination with FEMA on flood risk and mitigation."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "4.7.3 Action 3: Comprehensive Data",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "With Action 3, you see the NAIC using catastrophe model research and market data to track availability and affordability. The goal is to help state insurance regulators compare regions, identify trends, support mitigation priorities, and connect insurance conditions with hazards such as floods, storms, wildfire, and extreme heat."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "4.7.4 Action 4: Risk Mitigation",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Action 4 centers on mitigation. You should expect shared resilience tools, stronger grant support, and better risk communication so applicants and policyholders understand how losses can be reduced. The strategy also promotes partnerships and funding advocacy to improve community insurability before disasters happen, not only after."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "4.7.5 Action 5: Test Climate Resilience Scenarios",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Action 5 pushes climate risk into solvency review. You should understand that scenario testing, catastrophe models, and climate stress analysis can help state insurance regulators evaluate insurer strength, product availability, and future reliability. The strategy also encourages partnerships with universities and research centers to improve data and methods."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "4.8 The Next Steps",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Looking ahead, you should expect climate adaptation to involve more than insurance regulation alone. Useful next steps include stronger stormwater systems, nature-based shoreline protection, better land-use choices, updated building practices, and continued investment in dams, levees, roads, and transit. FEMA has also proposed a Homeowner Flood Form that could add flexibility, improve resilience, and make coverage easier for you to explain to clients. At the same time, climate science keeps changing, and each region faces different conditions. That means you will need current data, refined models, and practical communication. State insurance regulators, insurers, communities, and lawmakers all have roles. The clearest lesson is that climate risk is already reshaping flood exposure, insurance affordability, and recovery. To respond well, you and your clients need regular updates, better mitigation, and a continued focus on communities with the fewest resources."
      }
    ]
  }
]

