        "type": "text",
        "content": "The proposed **technical** updates would streamline reporting, require administrative appeals before lawsuits, and shorten the time to sue after an appeal decision. They would also reduce barriers when policyholders switch to private flood coverage. For you, these changes matter because they could affect claims handling timelines, documentation expectations, and how continuous coverage is preserved."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.4. The Need for Model Laws in the Private Market",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "As private flood coverage grows, **model laws** can help states create more consistent rules. You may work across jurisdictions, so uniform standards can make products easier to compare and regulate. The NAIC has encouraged private market development, and NCOIL adopted a model act in 2020 to help states build private residential flood frameworks."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.4.1 The National Association of Insurance Commissioners",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The **NAIC** has not issued a private flood model law, but it has given state insurance regulators practical guidance. In a 2019 paper, it highlighted three areas: legislative and regulatory changes, consumer information, and actions by agents and lenders. You should note its emphasis on flexible filing rules, clearer comparisons with NFIP coverage, public lists of private insurers, and stronger flood education so your clients better understand that flood risk exists well beyond mapped high-risk zones."
