        "content": "In 2020, **NCOIL** adopted the Private Primary Residential Flood Insurance Model Act. It gives states a framework for rates, forms, consumer notices, cancellations, surplus lines access, and insurer duties. For you, an important feature is its consumer notice requirement: applicants in Special Flood Hazard Areas must be told that NFIP coverage remains available and may include subsidized pricing."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.4.3 ISO Flood Insurance Programs",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**ISO** introduced private flood programs for commercial and personal lines to help insurers rate and underwrite these risks. The personal program uses territory-based loss costs and additional rating variables. The commercial program gives insurers more flexibility, including optional coverages such as business interruption and some basement-related losses that the NFIP does not typically provide."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.5 Mitigation at the Community and State Levels",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**Mitigation** is one of the clearest ways to reduce long-term flood loss and support affordability. FEMA offers hazard mitigation assistance grants to states, territories, tribes, and communities for projects before and after disasters. When you think about premium pressure, remember that stronger buildings, better drainage, and smarter planning can reduce future claims and improve resilience."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.5.1 Actions for State and Local Governments",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "State and local governments play a central **role** in administering many mitigation funds. They help identify priorities such as updated building codes, zoning rules, revolving loan funds, and tax incentives for flood-hardening measures. As you review community options, you should understand that these government actions can shape both flood exposure and the long-term cost of insurance."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.5.2 Options for Communities",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Communities have several **options**. Before disasters, they may pursue BRIC or Flood Mitigation Assistance grants. After disasters, they may use HMGP or HMGP Post-Fire, which can address later flooding, erosion, and mudflows after wildfire. Communities may also join the optional Community Rating System, where stronger floodplain management can earn NFIP premium discounts for most in-force policies."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.5.3 Safeguarding Tomorrow Revolving Loan Fund (RLF) Program",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The **Safeguarding Tomorrow Revolving Loan Fund** program gives states, territories, tribes, and Washington, D.C., capital to create low-interest loan funds for local mitigation projects. You should know that these loans are meant to help communities with limited resources, and FEMA aims to direct a meaningful share of benefits to under-resourced areas. Unlike many hazard mitigation grants, the program offers local governments more flexibility, can help satisfy nonfederal cost-share requirements, and does not require a benefit-cost analysis for each project."
      }
    ]
  }
