  {
    "heading": "1.1 Coverage",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**Flood insurance** is usually separate from a standard homeowners policy because flood damage is generally excluded. In practice, you will often see coverage issued through the National Flood Insurance Program, or NFIP, including policies sold by private carriers through the Write-Your-Own program. For this lesson, your goal is to understand what the NFIP covers, what limits apply, and how rates are set under Risk Rating 2.0."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**Flood** has a specific NFIP meaning. The loss must involve a general and temporary condition where water covers normally dry land, and it must affect at least two acres or at least two properties, one of which can be the insured property. Covered causes include overflow of inland or tidal waters, rapid surface water buildup or runoff, certain mudflows, and some shoreline collapse caused by flood-related erosion. If your client reports a wet basement from a failed sump pump, that does not meet the NFIP definition of flood."
      },
      {
        "type": "bullet_list",
        "items": [
          "**Building property** covers the dwelling, attached additions, and a detached garage, usually up to 10% of the building limit.",
          "**Personal property** covers enclosed, secured contents on the premises and may cover property moved to safety for up to 45 days.",
          "**Other coverages** can include debris removal, loss avoidance, moving property to safety, condominium loss assessments, and some pollution damage, depending on the form.",
          "**Increased cost of compliance (ICC)** helps pay added costs to meet current code, such as elevation, relocation, demolition, or approved floodproofing."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "important_callout",
        "content": "Separate deductibles apply to building coverage and contents coverage. If both are damaged in one covered flood, your client may have to satisfy two deductibles before policy benefits are paid."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "1.2 Coverage Limits",
    "body_paragraphs": [
