    "heading": "1.1 Coverage",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Most standard property policies do not pay for **flood** damage, so your client usually needs separate flood coverage. In practice, that coverage is often written through the National Flood Insurance Program, or NFIP, either directly or through a private insurer in the Write-Your-Own program. In this lesson, you will explain what the NFIP covers, the limits that apply, and how rates are set under Risk Rating 2.0."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "For NFIP purposes, a flood is a general and temporary condition where water covers normally dry land. The event must affect at least two acres or at least two properties, and one of those properties can be your client’s. Covered causes include overflow of inland or tidal waters, rapid runoff or surface water buildup, certain mudflows, and shoreline collapse caused by erosion from unusually high water. If your client’s basement fills because a sump pump fails, that loss does not meet the NFIP flood definition."
      },
      {
        "type": "bullet_list",
        "items": [
          "**Building property** covers the insured building, attached additions, some materials used for repair, and a detached garage up to 10% of the building limit.",
          "**Personal property** covers enclosed and secured contents on the premises and may also cover property moved to safety for up to 45 days.",
          "**Other coverages** can include debris removal, loss avoidance measures, moving property to safety, condominium loss assessments, and some pollution damage, depending on the policy form.",
          "**Increased cost of compliance (ICC)** helps pay costs to elevate, floodproof, relocate, or demolish a building when local ordinance or law requires it after a flood loss."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "important_callout",
        "content": "You should remember that separate deductibles usually apply to building coverage and contents coverage. If both are damaged in one covered flood, your client may have to satisfy both deductibles before payment begins."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "1.2 Coverage Limits",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "To qualify for **NFIP eligibility**, the property must be in a participating community or on federal land. A community joins by adopting floodplain management rules that meet state and federal program standards and by having an official flood map, such as a Flood Insurance Rate Map. During the Emergency Program, only lower limits are available. After the map is fully in effect and the community enters the Regular Program, broader limits apply."
      },
